.TH OPENAT 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
openat \- open a path relative to a directory descriptor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fcntl.h>
.PP
.BI "int openat(int " directory ", const char* " path ", int " flags ", mode_t " mode ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
For a relative path,
.B openat
uses the open directory descriptor as the starting point; use
.B AT_FDCWD
to start at the current working directory. An absolute path ignores the
directory descriptor. All four arguments are required; pass zero for
.I mode
when no creation permissions are needed. Mode and flags have the same
meaning as in
.BR open (3crunch).
The header also supplies
.BR O_DIRECTORY ,
.BR O_NOFOLLOW ,
and
.BR O_CLOEXEC .
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns a descriptor on success, leaving
.B errno
unchanged, or \-1 on failure with positive Linux
.B errno .
.SH NOTES
The interface targets Linux x86\-64; Crunch errno is process\-wide.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (3crunch),
.BR openat (2)
